 ### SVDAG3
-An optimized Sparse Voxel DAG structure using three child node pointer types (PT1: 8b, PT2: 16b, PT3: 32b) with an 8b address word length. It is identified as the most compact HDS configuration for all tested models and resolutions. It uses a 16b CHNM with no reserved bits. While it offers superior compression (1.88x to 3.06x compared to conventional SVDAG), it is slower to traverse than SVDAG1 (ratio 0.81 on Angel Lucy 4096^3) due to 8b alignment requiring shifting operations [S1, 5950-local-vs-global-addressing-in-svdag.md].
+An optimized Sparse Voxel DAG structure using three child node pointer types (PT1: 8b, PT2: 16b, PT3: 32b) with an 8b address word length [S1]. It is identified as the most compact HDS configuration for all tested models and resolutions [S1]. It uses a 16b CHNM with no reserved bits [S1]. While it offers superior compression (1.88x to 3.06x compared to conventional SVDAG), it is slower to traverse than SVDAG1 (ratio 0.81 on Angel Lucy 4096^3) due to 8b alignment requiring shifting operations [S1].
 
 ### Frequency-Based Compaction (FBC)
-An optimization that sorts nodes by referencing frequency and assigns addresses to utilize shorter pointer address spaces for frequently referenced nodes, keeping less frequent nodes in longer pointer address spaces. It requires sorting nodes in descending order of referencing frequency and assigning addresses in ascending order. It is advantageous to use separate addressing spaces for each HDS layer rather than a global continuous addressing space [S1, 5950-local-vs-global-addressing-in-svdag.md].
