 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Traversal is slower than fixed-length pointer schemes. Optimized SVDAGs using variable-length pointers are 15% to 25% slower than SVDAG1 (32b fixed) [S1]. For example, SVDAG3 traversal time is 0.81 times that of SVDAG1 for Angel Lucy 4096^3 [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Provides significant compression gains. Compression enhancement ranges from 1.88x (at 4096^3 resolution) to 3.06x (at 128^3 resolution) compared to conventional SVDAG HDS [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Maximizes compression for scenes of varying sizes by matching pointer length to address range. SVDAG3 is the most compact configuration for tested models [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Increased traversal overhead due to mixed alignment and decoding. Aligning CHNM to 32b increases binary size by a factor of 4, and large portions of the 32b address space may remain unused [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When memory footprint is critical and the scene size allows for efficient packing of frequently accessed nodes in shorter pointer ranges.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When traversal speed is the primary constraint and the scene size fits comfortably within a fixed 32b pointer scheme without significant waste [S1].
